=== Second Inquiry: Directions Against Entering into Temptation

#smallcaps[Having] seen the danger of entering into temptation, and having also uncovered the ways in which and the seasons when men usually do so, our second inquiry is this: what general directions may be given to preserve a soul from the condition that has been spoken of? And we see our Savior’s direction in the place mentioned before (Matt 26:41). He sums it all up in these two words, “Watch and pray.” I will labor a little to unfold them, and to show what is wrapped up and contained in them, both together and separately.

==== Watch and Pray, Taken Jointly

(1.) Included in them is a clear, #emph[abiding awareness of the great evil] there is in entering into temptation. What a man watches and prays against he regards as evil to him, and to be avoided by all means.

This, then, is the #emph[first] direction: #emph[Always bear in mind how great a danger it is for any soul to enter into temptation.]

It is a woeful thing to consider how lightly most people think of this. As long as men can keep themselves from sin itself in open action, they are content; they hardly aim at more. All sorts of men will venture on any temptation in the world at any time. How young men will throw themselves into any company, any society! At first they are delighted with #emph[evil company], and then with the #emph[evil of the company]! How useless are all warnings and exhortations to them to beware of such persons, who are debauched themselves, corrupters of others, and destroyers of souls! At first they will venture on the company while abhorring the thought of practicing its lewdness. But what is the outcome? Except for one here or there whom God snatches with a mighty hand from the jaws of destruction, they are all lost, and after a while they come to love the evil they abhorred at first. This open door to the ruin of souls is all too plain. And woeful experience makes it no less plain that it is almost impossible to fasten any fear or dread of temptation on many poor creatures who nevertheless profess a fear and abhorrence of sin. If only it were so with young men alone, those unaccustomed to the yoke of the Lord! But what sort of man is free from this folly in one thing or another? How many professors I have known who would plead for their #emph[liberty], as they called it! They could hear anything, everything, all sorts of men, all men. They would try all things, whether these came to them in the way of God or not. On that account they would run to hear and attend to everyone who broached false and abominable opinions, every seducer, though most of the saints had branded him as such. For they had their liberty to hear such a one, they could do it; but they hated the opinions as much as anyone. What has been the outcome? I hardly ever knew any of them come off without a wound; most have had their faith overthrown. Let no man, then, claim to fear sin if he does not fear the temptation to it. They are too closely related to be separated. Satan has put them so close together that it is very hard for any man to put them apart. The man who delights in the root does not hate the fruit.

When men see that certain ways, company, #emph[courses], business, studies, and #emph[aims] entangle them, make them cold and careless, act like water on coals to them, and make them unfit for even, universal, and constant obedience, sin lies at the door if they venture on them. It takes a tender frame of spirit to perform this duty, one aware of its own weakness and corruption, of the craft of Satan, of the evil of sin, and of the efficacy of temptation. And yet until we bring our hearts to this frame, through the considerations mentioned before or others like them that may be put forward, we will never free ourselves from sinful entanglements. Boldness toward temptation, springing from various pretexts, has, as is well known, ruined countless professors in these days, and it still goes on throwing many down from their excellence. I have not the least hope of a more fruitful profession among us until I see more fear of temptation. Sin will not seem great or heavy for long to anyone to whom temptations seem light or small.

This is the first thing wrapped up in this general direction: what is required of us is the daily exercise of our thoughts with an awareness of the great danger that lies in entering into temptation. Grief to the Spirit of God, disquiet in our own souls, loss of peace, and danger to our eternal welfare lie at the door. If the soul is not won over to keep this direction, all that follows will be of no value. Temptation despised will conquer. And if the heart is made tender and watchful here, half the work of securing good conduct is done. Let no man go any further who does not resolve to make use of this direction by keeping it conscientiously every day.

(2.) There is this in it too: it is #emph[not in our own power] to keep and preserve ourselves from entering into temptation. That is why we are to pray to be preserved from it, because we cannot save ourselves.

This is another means of preservation. We have no strength to resist a temptation when it does come, when we have entered into it; we will fall under it unless God supplies us with sufficient grace. In the same way, to reckon that we have no power or wisdom to keep ourselves from entering into temptation, but must be kept by the power and wisdom of God, is a preserving principle (1 Pet 1:5). In all things we are “kept by God’s power.” Our Savior teaches us this, not only by directing us to pray that we will not be led into temptation, but also by praying for us himself that we may be kept from it: “I am not asking that You take them out of the world, but that You keep them from the evil” (John 17:15). That is, from the temptations of the world to evil, to sin. ἐκ τοῦ πονηροῦ, “out of the evil” that is in the world, which is temptation, for that is all that is evil in the world; or else from the evil one, who makes use of the world for temptation. Christ prays to his Father to keep us, and teaches us to pray that we may be kept in this way. It is not, then, a thing in our own power. The ways in which we enter into temptation are so many, so various, and so imperceptible; the means of it so efficacious and powerful; its entrances so deceitful, subtle, unnoticed, and plausible; our weakness and our unwatchfulness so beyond words, that we cannot keep or preserve ourselves from it in the least. We lack both the wisdom and the power for this work.

Let the heart, then, talk with itself like this: “#emph[I] am poor and weak. #emph[Satan] is subtle, cunning, and powerful, constantly watching for advantages against my soul. The #emph[world] is earnest and pressing, and full of plausible pleas, countless pretexts, and ways of deceit. My #emph[own corruption] is violent and in a tumult, enticing, entangling, conceiving sin, and warring in me, against me. The #emph[occasions] and advantages of temptation are countless, in all that I do or suffer, in all my business and in all the persons I deal with. The #emph[first beginnings] of temptation are unnoticed and plausible, so that, if I am left to myself, I will not know that I am ensnared until my bonds are made strong and sin has gained ground in my heart. Therefore I will rely on God alone for preservation, and I will continually look up to him for it.” This will make the soul always commit itself to the care of God and rest itself on him, and do nothing, undertake nothing, and so on, without asking his counsel. So a double advantage will come from keeping this direction, and both are of special use for the soul’s preservation from the evil it fears:

\[1.\] It engages the grace and compassion of God, who has called the #emph[fatherless] and the #emph[helpless] to rest on him. No soul ever lacked supplies that, sensing its need, rolled itself on him because of his gracious invitation.

\[2.\] It #emph[keeps] the soul in a frame that is useful for its preservation in various ways. The man who looks to God for help in a right manner is both aware of his danger and conscientiously careful in using the means to preserve himself. How important these two things are in this case may easily be understood by those whose hearts are exercised in these things.

(3.) This also is in it: put #emph[faith] to work on the #emph[promise] of God for preservation. To believe that he will preserve us is a means of preservation. For God will certainly do this, or make a way for us to escape out of temptation if we fall into it while in such a believing frame. We are to pray for what God has promised. Our requests are to be governed by his promises and commands, which are of the same extent. Faith lays hold of the promises, and so finds relief in this case. James teaches us this in chapter 1:5–7. What we lack we must “ask God” for; but we “must ask in faith,” for otherwise we “should not expect to receive anything from the Lord.” This, then, is also in our Savior’s direction: that we put faith to work on the promises of God for our preservation from temptation. He has promised that he will keep us in all our ways; that we will be directed in a way in which, fools though we are, we will not go astray (Isa 35:8); that he will lead us, guide us, and deliver us from the evil one. Set faith to work on these promises of God, and expect a good and comforting outcome. It is not easy to conceive what a train of graces goes with faith when it goes out to meet Christ in the promises, nor what power for the preservation of the soul lies in this. But I have spoken of this elsewhere.#footnote[Mortification of Sin in Believers, ch. 14.]

==== Pray

(4.) Weigh these things #emph[separately], and first take prayer into consideration. To pray that we will not enter into temptation is a means of preserving us from it. All men who know anything of these things speak glorious things of this #emph[duty]\; and yet the truth is that not half of its excellence, power, and efficacy is known. It is not my business to speak of it in general. But this I say, for my present purpose: let the man who would be little in temptation be much in prayer. Prayer calls in the fitting help and aid that is laid up for us in Christ (Heb 4:16). It puts our souls into a frame of opposition to every temptation. When Paul had given instruction for taking up “the full armor of God,” so that we may resist and stand in a time of temptation, he adds this general close to the whole: “Pray in the Spirit at all times, with every kind of prayer and petition. To this end, keep watch with all perseverance in your#super[+] prayers” (Eph 6:18).

Without this all the rest will have no efficacy for the end in view. So consider what weight he lays on it. “Pray … at all times”: that is, at all times and seasons, or always be ready and prepared to carry out that duty (Luke 18:1; Eph 6:18). “In the Spirit … with every kind of prayer and petition”: putting forth to God all kinds of desires that suit our condition, according to his will, in which we are helped by the Spirit. “To this end, keep watch”: so that we are not turned aside by anything whatever; and that not for a little while, but “with all perseverance,” a continuance drawn out to the utmost. So we will stand. The soul framed like this is in a sure position; and this is one of the means without which this work will not be done. If we do not abide in prayer, we will abide in cursed temptations. Let this, then, be another direction: abide in prayer, and that expressly for this purpose, that we “will not enter into temptation.” Let this be one part of our daily wrestling with God: that he would preserve our souls and keep our hearts and our ways, so that we are not entangled; that his good and wise providence will so order our ways and affairs that no pressing temptation befalls us; that he would give us diligence, carefulness, and watchfulness over our own ways. So we will be delivered when others are held fast with the cords of their own folly.
